\chapter{A complete program and the language contract}
The following program imports three native packages, builds a collection,
passes a closure into a higher-order operation, and formats its result.
It is a compact example of MuLL's intended balance: a small core and reusable
library operations.

\begin{mullcode}
val P <- G:LoadLib("printf") or G:DieWith("$E?");
val L <- G:LoadLib("list") or G:DieWith("$E?");
val T <- G:LoadLib("text") or G:DieWith("$E?");
val selected <- L:Filter(L:Range(1,11), fn(x){ return x % 2 == 0; });
val labels <- L:Map(selected, fn(x){ return P:Format("n={}",x); });
val sum <- L:Fold(selected, 0, fn(a,x){ return a+x; });
P:Println(T:Join(labels, ", "));
P:Printf("sum={}\n", sum);
G:Assert(sum == 30);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Operational contract}
By default a run permits one million evaluation steps, 128 nested script
calls, 100,000 cumulative frame allocations, and four MiB of source per file.
The host can change those limits. Parsing and expression evaluation also
have fixed nesting guards. A native callback executes host code; the step
counter counts interpreter work rather than elapsed time or native memory.

A runtime retains top-level bindings, captured environments, registered native
values, and cached packages. Syntax and static semantics are validated before effects begin.
Evaluation errors preserve effects already performed. Package initialization
is isolated from application bindings but uses the same host services.

\section{Extending deliberately}
The reference grammar and library tables in \api{mull/README.md} summarize
the implemented language. New services normally belong in native packages;
new syntax belongs in both hosts, MuLLIER, and shared conformance tests.
The current language has no classes, exception-catching syntax, dynamic shared
library ABI, or compiler backend. Its complete execution model is the lexical,
bounded tree interpreter described here, preceded by the MuLLIER passes in the
following chapters.
